  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a receptor tyrosine kinase signaling network at the cell membrane and maps targeted cancer drugs onto it. Several extracellular growth factors, including FGF, PDGF, VEGF, EGF, HGF, and IGF, bind their corresponding receptors, while the HER family receptors are also highlighted. Percentages next to many receptors and downstream proteins appear to indicate how often these alterations occur.\n\nSignals from these receptors converge mainly on two major intracellular pathways: the RAS–RAF–MEK–ERK cascade and the PI3K–PDK1–AKT–mTOR pathway. These pathways then feed into nuclear transcriptional machinery, where factors such as p53 and HDAC are shown, ultimately affecting gene transcription. The biological consequences shown at the bottom include increased proliferation, survival/anti-apoptosis, metastasis, and angiogenesis, along with upregulation of genes such as Cyclin D1, MYC, VEGF, and MCL1.\n\nA central message is therapeutic targeting across many levels of the pathway. Monoclonal antibodies are shown acting at receptor level, including drugs against VEGF, EGFR, HER2/HER3, HGF, and IGFR, while small-molecule inhibitors target receptors or downstream nodes such as FGFR, VEGFR, EGFR, RAS, RAF, mTOR, and HDAC. Overall, the diagram presents an integrated view of oncogenic signaling and intervention points.",
